<Folder><name>1 — Strong geographic identifications</name><open>1</open><visibility>1</visibility>
<Placemark>
<name>Jerusalem — the historical setting</name>
<styleUrl>#category-1</styleUrl>
<Snippet maxLines="4">High for the city; not an event-specific point. All four Gospels repeatedly place Jesus in Jerusalem, particularly at pilgrimage festivals and during the final week. The marker is centered on the ancient-city area rather than on a single building.</Snippet>
<description><![CDATA[ASSESSMENT
High for the city; not an event-specific point

RELEVANT PASSAGES
Luke 2:41-52; Mark 11-16; John 2:13-25

WHY THE MARKER IS HERE
All four Gospels repeatedly place Jesus in Jerusalem, particularly at pilgrimage festivals and during the final week. The marker is centered on the ancient-city area rather than on a single building.

WHAT REMAINS UNCERTAIN
The extent, walls, streets, and place names of first-century Jerusalem differ substantially from the modern Old City. A modern pin cannot represent the whole Herodian city.

SCRIPTURE LINKS
- Luke 2:41-52: https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Luke+2%3A41-52&version=NRSVUE
- Mark 11-16: https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Mark+11-16&version=NRSVUE
- John 2:13-25: https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=John+2%3A13-25&version=NRSVUE

REFERENCES
- OpenBible.info: Jerusalem coordinates and atlas bibliography: https://www.openbible.info/geo/modern/m66c5b8/jerusalem

Coordinates mark a modern site or representative point; they do not claim centimeter-level location of an ancient event.]]></description>
