<Placemark>
<name>Bethphage — traditional church area</name>
<styleUrl>#category-2</styleUrl>
<Snippet maxLines="4">Moderate-to-low. The Synoptics place Bethphage by Bethany and the Mount of Olives on the Jerusalem approach. The traditional site at at-Tur fits the broad route and has an old pilgrimage history.</Snippet>
<description><![CDATA[ASSESSMENT
Moderate-to-low

RELEVANT PASSAGES
Matthew 21:1-11; Mark 11:1-11; Luke 19:28-40

WHY THE MARKER IS HERE
The Synoptics place Bethphage by Bethany and the Mount of Olives on the Jerusalem approach. The traditional site at at-Tur fits the broad route and has an old pilgrimage history.

WHAT REMAINS UNCERTAIN
The ancient village has not been positively identified, and even the Synoptic wording leaves its precise relation to Bethany uncertain. The church marks tradition, not a demonstrated stable or mounting stone.

SCRIPTURE LINKS
- Matthew 21:1-11: https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Matthew+21%3A1-11&version=NRSVUE
- Mark 11:1-11: https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Mark+11%3A1-11&version=NRSVUE
- Luke 19:28-40: https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Luke+19%3A28-40&version=NRSVUE

REFERENCES
- Oxford Archaeological Guide discussion of Bethphage: https://ndl.ethernet.edu.et/bitstream/123456789/52968/1/18.Murphy-O%E2%80%99Connor.pdf

Coordinates mark a modern site or representative point; they do not claim centimeter-level location of an ancient event.]]></description>
