<Placemark>
<name>Tomb of Lazarus — traditional cave</name>
<styleUrl>#category-2</styleUrl>
<Snippet maxLines="4">Village secure; particular tomb merely possible. The shown tomb lies within securely identified Bethany and was being pointed out by the fourth century. It preserves a long, continuous pilgrimage tradition.</Snippet>
<description><![CDATA[ASSESSMENT
Village secure; particular tomb merely possible

RELEVANT PASSAGES
John 11:17-44

WHY THE MARKER IS HERE
The shown tomb lies within securely identified Bethany and was being pointed out by the fourth century. It preserves a long, continuous pilgrimage tradition.

WHAT REMAINS UNCERTAIN
The cave has been altered, and there is no inscription or decisive excavation connecting it to Lazarus. Even sources favorable to Bethany's identification distinguish the town from the much weaker claim for this exact tomb.

SCRIPTURE LINKS
- John 11:17-44: https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=John+11%3A17-44&version=NRSVUE

REFERENCES
- Hebrew University gazetteer: Bethany / el-Eizariya / Lazarion: https://dig.corps-cmhl.huji.ac.il/literary_sources/bethany-el-eizariya-lazarion-2
- Tomb of Lazarus: history and limits of the identification: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tomb_of_Lazarus

Coordinates mark a modern site or representative point; they do not claim centimeter-level location of an ancient event.]]></description>
