<Placemark>
<name>Herod&#x27;s western palace — leading praetorium candidate</name>
<styleUrl>#category-2</styleUrl>
<Snippet maxLines="4">Moderate; leading scholarly proposal. Roman governors commonly occupied the client king&#x27;s palace when visiting Jerusalem. Literary and archaeological arguments therefore place Pilate&#x27;s praetorium at Herod&#x27;s palace near today&#x27;s Citadel/Jaffa Gate, rather than at Antonia.</Snippet>
<description><![CDATA[ASSESSMENT
Moderate; leading scholarly proposal

RELEVANT PASSAGES
Mark 15:1-20; John 18:28-19:16

WHY THE MARKER IS HERE
Roman governors commonly occupied the client king's palace when visiting Jerusalem. Literary and archaeological arguments therefore place Pilate's praetorium at Herod's palace near today's Citadel/Jaffa Gate, rather than at Antonia.

WHAT REMAINS UNCERTAIN
The palace complex is incompletely preserved and the Gospels do not name the building. Antonia remains a major pilgrimage tradition and some scholars have proposed other palatial locations.

SCRIPTURE LINKS
- Mark 15:1-20: https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Mark+15%3A1-20&version=NRSVUE
- John 18:28-19:16: https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=John+18%3A28-19%3A16&version=NRSVUE

REFERENCES
- Routledge Encyclopedia of the Historical Jesus: praetorium discussion: https://azahari.blog/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/encyclopedia-of-historical-jesus.pdf
- Study of trial-location and Passion-route logistics: https://scholarsarchive.byu.edu/studiaantiqua/vol22/iss1/5/

Coordinates mark a modern site or representative point; they do not claim centimeter-level location of an ancient event.]]></description>
