<Placemark>
<name>Church of the Holy Sepulchre — Golgotha and tomb tradition</name>
<styleUrl>#category-2</styleUrl>
<Snippet maxLines="4">Plausible and ancient; not certain. The site was a disused quarry and burial area outside the likely first-century wall. Constantine&#x27;s fourth-century complex replaced a Roman-period structure, suggesting an earlier local memory rather than a site selected merely because it looked like a hill.</Snippet>
<description><![CDATA[ASSESSMENT
Plausible and ancient; not certain

RELEVANT PASSAGES
Mark 15:20-16:8; John 19:17-20:18; Hebrews 13:12

WHY THE MARKER IS HERE
The site was a disused quarry and burial area outside the likely first-century wall. Constantine's fourth-century complex replaced a Roman-period structure, suggesting an earlier local memory rather than a site selected merely because it looked like a hill.

WHAT REMAINS UNCERTAIN
The precise course of Jerusalem's Second Wall remains debated, and archaeology cannot identify Jesus's individual cross or tomb. Some scholarship distinguishes the broader quarry of Golgotha from the exact points now shown inside the church.

SCRIPTURE LINKS
- Mark 15:20-16:8: https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Mark+15%3A20-16%3A8&version=NRSVUE
- John 19:17-20:18: https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=John+19%3A17-20%3A18&version=NRSVUE
- Hebrews 13:12: https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Hebrews+13%3A12&version=NRSVUE

REFERENCES
- Cambridge, New Testament Studies: reconsidering Golgotha: https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/new-testament-studies/article/abs/golgotha-a-reconsideration-of-the-evidence-for-the-sites-of-jesus-crucifixion-and-burial/EBF49AC4C712692B1834307624C143F9
- Biblical Archaeology Society: evidence concerning Golgotha: https://www.biblicalarchaeology.org/daily/biblical-sites-places/jerusalem/where-is-golgotha-where-jesus-was-crucified/

Coordinates mark a modern site or representative point; they do not claim centimeter-level location of an ancient event.]]></description>
