<Placemark>
<name>Chapel of the Ascension — traditional summit site</name>
<styleUrl>#category-3</styleUrl>
<Snippet maxLines="4">Ancient tradition; exact point unknown. Acts places the ascension on the Mount of Olives, a Sabbath day&#x27;s journey from Jerusalem. The summit shrine preserves an old Christian commemoration.</Snippet>
<description><![CDATA[ASSESSMENT
Ancient tradition; exact point unknown

RELEVANT PASSAGES
Luke 24:50-53; Acts 1:6-12

WHY THE MARKER IS HERE
Acts places the ascension on the Mount of Olives, a Sabbath day's journey from Jerusalem. The summit shrine preserves an old Christian commemoration.

WHAT REMAINS UNCERTAIN
Luke mentions Bethany, Acts the Mount of Olives, and neither identifies a summit footprint. The chapel's claimed footprint is devotional, not demonstrable archaeology.

SCRIPTURE LINKS
- Luke 24:50-53: https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Luke+24%3A50-53&version=NRSVUE
- Acts 1:6-12: https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Acts+1%3A6-12&version=NRSVUE

REFERENCES
- OpenBible.info: Jerusalem coordinates and atlas bibliography: https://www.openbible.info/geo/modern/m66c5b8/jerusalem

Coordinates mark a modern site or representative point; they do not claim centimeter-level location of an ancient event.]]></description>
