<Placemark>
<name>Antonia Fortress — traditional trial vicinity</name>
<styleUrl>#category-3</styleUrl>
<Snippet maxLines="4">Fortress location approximate; trial claim disputed. Antonia guarded the Temple&#x27;s northwest corner and became the traditional eastern starting point for Jesus&#x27;s trial and the Via Dolorosa.</Snippet>
<description><![CDATA[ASSESSMENT
Fortress location approximate; trial claim disputed

RELEVANT PASSAGES
John 18:28-19:16

WHY THE MARKER IS HERE
Antonia guarded the Temple's northwest corner and became the traditional eastern starting point for Jesus's trial and the Via Dolorosa.

WHAT REMAINS UNCERTAIN
There is no firm archaeological link between Antonia and Pilate's praetorium. Many modern scholars favor Herod's western palace, which would produce a very different Passion route.

SCRIPTURE LINKS
- John 18:28-19:16: https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=John+18%3A28-19%3A16&version=NRSVUE

REFERENCES
- Routledge Encyclopedia of the Historical Jesus: praetorium discussion: https://azahari.blog/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/encyclopedia-of-historical-jesus.pdf
- Study of trial-location and Passion-route logistics: https://scholarsarchive.byu.edu/studiaantiqua/vol22/iss1/5/

Coordinates mark a modern site or representative point; they do not claim centimeter-level location of an ancient event.]]></description>
