<Placemark>
<name>Ecce Homo / Lithostrotos pavement tradition</name>
<styleUrl>#category-3</styleUrl>
<Snippet maxLines="4">Devotional identification; pavement largely later Roman. The Sisters of Zion convent preserves paving and an arch long associated with Pilate&#x27;s judgment and &#x27;Ecce Homo.&#x27; It anchors the current devotional Via Dolorosa.</Snippet>
<description><![CDATA[ASSESSMENT
Devotional identification; pavement largely later Roman

RELEVANT PASSAGES
John 19:5-13

WHY THE MARKER IS HERE
The Sisters of Zion convent preserves paving and an arch long associated with Pilate's judgment and 'Ecce Homo.' It anchors the current devotional Via Dolorosa.

WHAT REMAINS UNCERTAIN
Much of the visible pavement and arch belong to Aelia Capitolina after 70 CE, too late for Jesus's trial. The identification also depends on the disputed Antonia-praetorium theory.

SCRIPTURE LINKS
- John 19:5-13: https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=John+19%3A5-13&version=NRSVUE

REFERENCES
- Routledge Encyclopedia of the Historical Jesus: praetorium discussion: https://azahari.blog/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/encyclopedia-of-historical-jesus.pdf
- Study of trial-location and Passion-route logistics: https://scholarsarchive.byu.edu/studiaantiqua/vol22/iss1/5/

Coordinates mark a modern site or representative point; they do not claim centimeter-level location of an ancient event.]]></description>
