<Placemark>
<name>Garden Tomb — nineteenth-century alternative</name>
<styleUrl>#category-3</styleUrl>
<Snippet maxLines="4">Low as Jesus&#x27;s tomb; important modern devotional site. The garden-like setting, rock-cut tomb, and nearby cliff suggested an evocative alternative to the Holy Sepulchre for some Protestant visitors beginning in the nineteenth century.</Snippet>
<description><![CDATA[ASSESSMENT
Low as Jesus's tomb; important modern devotional site

RELEVANT PASSAGES
John 19:38-42

WHY THE MARKER IS HERE
The garden-like setting, rock-cut tomb, and nearby cliff suggested an evocative alternative to the Holy Sepulchre for some Protestant visitors beginning in the nineteenth century.

WHAT REMAINS UNCERTAIN
Archaeological dating generally places the tomb centuries earlier than Jesus and later reuse does not make it a newly cut first-century tomb. Its claim is substantially weaker than the Holy Sepulchre tradition.

SCRIPTURE LINKS
- John 19:38-42: https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=John+19%3A38-42&version=NRSVUE

REFERENCES
- Cambridge, New Testament Studies: reconsidering Golgotha: https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/new-testament-studies/article/abs/golgotha-a-reconsideration-of-the-evidence-for-the-sites-of-jesus-crucifixion-and-burial/EBF49AC4C712692B1834307624C143F9
- Review of Holy Sepulchre and Garden Tomb evidence: https://rsc.byu.edu/vol-4-no-1-2003/revisiting-golgotha-garden-tomb

Coordinates mark a modern site or representative point; they do not claim centimeter-level location of an ancient event.]]></description>
