<Placemark>
<name>Catholic Shepherds&#x27; Field — Beit Sahour</name>
<styleUrl>#category-3</styleUrl>
<Snippet maxLines="4">Regional tradition; exact field unknown. This pin is placed on the Franciscan Shepherds&#x27; Field Chapel (Gloria in excelsis Deo), the location shown to visitors as the Catholic traditional site. The rolling country east of Bethlehem suits Luke&#x27;s general setting, and the compound preserves caves and an old commemoration of the shepherds.</Snippet>
<description><![CDATA[ASSESSMENT
Regional tradition; exact field unknown

RELEVANT PASSAGES
Luke 2:8-20

WHY THE MARKER IS HERE
This pin is placed on the Franciscan Shepherds' Field Chapel (Gloria in excelsis Deo), the location shown to visitors as the Catholic traditional site. The rolling country east of Bethlehem suits Luke's general setting, and the compound preserves caves and an old commemoration of the shepherds.

WHAT REMAINS UNCERTAIN
Luke names no village, owner, or parcel. This is one of several competing fields around Beit Sahour and cannot be singled out archaeologically.

SCRIPTURE LINKS
- Luke 2:8-20: https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Luke+2%3A8-20&version=NRSVUE

REFERENCES
- Bible Odyssey / HarperCollins Bible Dictionary: Bethlehem: https://www.bibleodyssey.org/dictionary/bethlehem/
- Franciscan Shepherds' Field chapel and mapped coordinates: https://www.octagonproject.org/news/the-shepherds%E2%80%99-field---franciscan

Coordinates mark a modern site or representative point; they do not claim centimeter-level location of an ancient event.]]></description>
