<Placemark>
<name>Ein Karem — Church of the Visitation</name>
<styleUrl>#category-3</styleUrl>
<Snippet maxLines="4">Strong later tradition; event site unproved. The hillside church commemorates Mary&#x27;s visit to Elizabeth and the Magnificat within the traditional Ein Karem landscape.</Snippet>
<description><![CDATA[ASSESSMENT
Strong later tradition; event site unproved

RELEVANT PASSAGES
Luke 1:39-56

WHY THE MARKER IS HERE
The hillside church commemorates Mary's visit to Elizabeth and the Magnificat within the traditional Ein Karem landscape.

WHAT REMAINS UNCERTAIN
Luke names neither Ein Karem nor a particular house, saying only 'a town in the hill country of Judea.' The church is a devotional localization.

SCRIPTURE LINKS
- Luke 1:39-56: https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Luke+1%3A39-56&version=NRSVUE

REFERENCES
- Church of the Visitation and the non-specific wording of Luke: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Church_of_the_Visitation

Coordinates mark a modern site or representative point; they do not claim centimeter-level location of an ancient event.]]></description>
<ExtendedData><Data name="Assessment"><displayName>Assessment</displayName><value>Strong later tradition; event site unproved</value></Data><Data name="Passages"><displayName>Passages</displayName><value>Luke 1:39-56</value></Data><Data name="Why here 1/2"><displayName>Why here 1/2</displayName><value>The hillside church commemorates Mary&#x27;s visit to Elizabeth and</value></Data><Data name="Why here 2/2"><displayName>Why here 2/2</displayName><value>the Magnificat within the traditional Ein Karem landscape.</value></Data><Data name="Uncertainty 1/3"><displayName>Uncertainty 1/3</displayName><value>Luke names neither Ein Karem nor a particular house, saying only</value></Data><Data name="Uncertainty 2/3"><displayName>Uncertainty 2/3</displayName><value>&#x27;a town in the hill country of Judea.&#x27; The church is a</value></Data><Data name="Uncertainty 3/3"><displayName>Uncertainty 3/3</displayName><value>devotional localization.</value></Data><Data name="Source 1"><displayName>Source 1</displayName><value>Church of the Visitation and the non-specific wording of Luke</value></Data><Data name="Source 1 URL — click to copy"><displayName>Source 1 URL — click to copy</displayName><value>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Church_of_the_Visitation</value></Data></ExtendedData>
<Point><coordinates>35.157860,31.764580,0</coordinates></Point>
</Placemark>
<Placemark>
<name>Herodium — Herod the Great context</name>
<styleUrl>#category-3</styleUrl>
<Snippet maxLines="4">High for Herod&#x27;s palace; indirect connection to Jesus. Herodium was Herod the Great&#x27;s palace-fortress and later burial place, visibly dominating the region southeast of Bethlehem. It supplies unusually concrete political and monumental context for Matthew&#x27;s infancy narrative.</Snippet>
<description><![CDATA[ASSESSMENT
High for Herod's palace; indirect connection to Jesus

RELEVANT PASSAGES
Matthew 2:1-18

WHY THE MARKER IS HERE
Herodium was Herod the Great's palace-fortress and later burial place, visibly dominating the region southeast of Bethlehem. It supplies unusually concrete political and monumental context for Matthew's infancy narrative.

WHAT REMAINS UNCERTAIN
No Gospel says Jesus, Mary, Joseph, or the Magi visited Herodium. This is contextual, not an event marker.

SCRIPTURE LINKS
- Matthew 2:1-18: https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Matthew+2%3A1-18&version=NRSVUE

REFERENCES
- Bible Odyssey / HarperCollins Bible Dictionary: Bethlehem: https://www.bibleodyssey.org/dictionary/bethlehem/

Coordinates mark a modern site or representative point; they do not claim centimeter-level location of an ancient event.]]></description>
<ExtendedData><Data name="Assessment"><displayName>Assessment</displayName><value>High for Herod&#x27;s palace; indirect connection to Jesus</value></Data><Data name="Passages"><displayName>Passages</displayName><value>Matthew 2:1-18</value></Data><Data name="Why here 1/4"><displayName>Why here 1/4</displayName><value>Herodium was Herod the Great&#x27;s palace-fortress and later burial</value></Data><Data name="Why here 2/4"><displayName>Why here 2/4</displayName><value>place, visibly dominating the region southeast of Bethlehem. It</value></Data><Data name="Why here 3/4"><displayName>Why here 3/4</displayName><value>supplies unusually concrete political and monumental context for</value></Data><Data name="Why here 4/4"><displayName>Why here 4/4</displayName><value>Matthew&#x27;s infancy narrative.</value></Data><Data name="Uncertainty 1/2"><displayName>Uncertainty 1/2</displayName><value>No Gospel says Jesus, Mary, Joseph, or the Magi visited</value></Data><Data name="Uncertainty 2/2"><displayName>Uncertainty 2/2</displayName><value>Herodium. This is contextual, not an event marker.</value></Data><Data name="Source 1"><displayName>Source 1</displayName><value>Bible Odyssey / HarperCollins Bible Dictionary: Bethlehem</value></Data><Data name="Source 1 URL — click to copy"><displayName>Source 1 URL — click to copy</displayName><value>https://www.bibleodyssey.org/dictionary/bethlehem/</value></Data></ExtendedData>
