<Placemark>
<name>Emmaus candidate — el-Qubeibeh</name>
<styleUrl>#category-4</styleUrl>
<Snippet maxLines="4">Medieval tradition; disputed. El-Qubeibeh lies northwest of Jerusalem at a broadly plausible walking distance and became the Franciscan Emmaus tradition in the later medieval period.</Snippet>
<description><![CDATA[ASSESSMENT
Medieval tradition; disputed

RELEVANT PASSAGES
Luke 24:13-35

WHY THE MARKER IS HERE
El-Qubeibeh lies northwest of Jerusalem at a broadly plausible walking distance and became the Franciscan Emmaus tradition in the later medieval period.

WHAT REMAINS UNCERTAIN
The identification is late and lacks a convincing ancient name connection. It is one candidate among several rather than the established village.

SCRIPTURE LINKS
- Luke 24:13-35: https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Luke+24%3A13-35&version=NRSVUE

REFERENCES
- OpenBible.info: six proposed identifications of Emmaus: https://www.openbible.info/geo/ancient/ae7274b/emmaus

Coordinates mark a modern site or representative point; they do not claim centimeter-level location of an ancient event.]]></description>
